,"label":"Bound the sine and attain the bound","methodDirectNodeIds":{"m2":["cnm_h029","cnm_h066","cnm_h018"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h029","cnm_h066","cnm_h018","cnm_h060"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A: necessarily x<=0 or x>=4; the exact attainable set is x<0 or x>=4","reason":"Rearrangement gives y^2-xy+x=0. Its discriminant requires x^2-4x>=0, proving A. At x=0 the only candidate is y=0, which the original equation excludes.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":["Choice A states a valid necessary bound but includes the unattainable boundary value x=0."],"questionId":"1957_AMC_12_Q45","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, a maximum value of -1","reason":"If f denotes the expression, then f+1=x²/(2x-2)≤0 because x<1. Equality holds at x=0, inside the interval, so the maximum is -1. As x approaches 1 from below, f tends to negative infinity.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1958_AMC_12_Q46","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_156","cnm_096"],"id":"compare-with-minus-one","label":"Compare directly with -1","methodDirectNodeIds":{"compare-with-minus-one":["cnm_h029","cnm_h019","cnm_161"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h029","cnm_h019","cnm_161","cnm_156","cnm_096"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 2","reason":"Subtracting 2 from the expression gives (2x-1)^2/[6(1+x)]≥0. Equality occurs at x=1/2, so the minimum is 2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1965_AMC_12_Q34","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 20<P<30","reason":"(x-2)(x-3)<0 gives 2<x<3. On this interval P=(x+2)(x+3) is strictly increasing, with endpoint values 20 and 30, neither attained.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1967_AMC_12_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_150","cnm_096"],"id":"solution:positive-factor-bounds:474d3e65bc02","label":"Factor and bound positive factors","methodDirectNodeIds":{"positive-factor-bounds":["cnm_h024","cnm_h029","cnm_h018"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h024","cnm_h029","cnm_h018","cnm_150","cnm_096"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: 6","reason":"Let t=x+1/x and u=x³+1/x³=t³-3t. The numerator is t⁶-u² and the denominator is t³+u>0, so f(x)=t³-u=3(x+1/x). This is at least 6, with equality at x=1.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1979_AMC_12_Q29","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_146","cnm_148","cnm_156"],"id":"1979_AMC_12_Q29_m1","label":"Factor before finding the minimum","methodDirectNodeIds":{"1979_AMC_12_Q29_m1":["cnm_162","cnm_h029","cnm_152","cnm_153","cnm_096"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_162","cnm_h029","cnm_152","cnm_153","cnm_096","cnm_146","cnm_148","cnm_156"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 2","reason":"On [2,3], f(x)=2x-4; on [3,4], f(x)=8-2x; and on [4,8], f(x)=0. Thus the minimum is 0 and the maximum is 2, whose sum is 2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1982_AMC_12_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C (2sqrt(3))","reason":"The common domain is [6,8]. There, 8x-x^2=16-(x-4)^2 is at most 12, and the second radical is nonnegative. Thus f(x) is at most sqrt(12), with equality at x=6.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1993_AMC_12_Q26","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[]},{"additi